{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377966} {:answer/id #uuid "66f4d7cd-145c-5afb-9d30-2523988c4b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66f4d7cd-145c-5afb-9d30-2523988c4b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>{s}_{0}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377967} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cb04070-9267-584f-84bc-d6fb0c152165" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "701f8e5e-1bab-52b4-a09e-74d12b5fc2d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-331808" :question/problem "Consider a function $d(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L{d(t)}=D(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L{{e}^{-9t}d(t)}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-9t}d(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-9t}d(t)} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "766b78e4-f110-58e0-9552-bb278d446a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s-3}{(s-3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377970} {:answer/id #uuid "83ad3a68-b942-5451-a902-12702fcf6a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377971} {:answer/id #uuid "cab77f13-6b25-5374-ba39-8e45dc00fce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377972} {:answer/id #uuid "69bab47c-29b4-5e05-ad6c-0b0715462084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377973} {:answer/id #uuid "35e01a65-6626-577d-89fb-a919caf9c064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s-3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83ad3a68-b942-5451-a902-12702fcf6a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377971} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb9fc2cd-597a-5a7b-a29a-e6ba2e32c454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6647987c-4f82-5d47-9637-0313d4fe5f69" :question/math-academy-id "q-331860" :question/problem "Given that $L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34856c10-dd9d-5c48-804d-45598e03059f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377977} {:answer/id #uuid "65826f02-f646-5e07-8082-27ac9329abec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10s}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377978} {:answer/id #uuid "a48fd826-c98e-53bd-9b30-fbe9fb3fd33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377979} {:answer/id #uuid "26155025-3f76-5904-8001-8ad7513f3762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(s+3)}{((s+3)^{2}+25)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377980} {:answer/id #uuid "18cf9bb1-4d7f-5d59-8136-a44694313771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(s-3)}{(s-3)^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a48fd826-c98e-53bd-9b30-fbe9fb3fd33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377979} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8447e40-5830-5850-bec3-791bd5a11b42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "49d7b22a-7950-5b4c-ad1d-b818abc9bc86" :question/math-academy-id "q-331864" :question/problem "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{2\\omega s}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}=\\frac{2(5)s}{({s}^{2}+{5}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{2(5)(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+{5}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c211398-1c03-5d79-bc88-75ce8785ac37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377984} {:answer/id #uuid "302eba65-8b01-54a8-85f5-11e0f4898873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-4}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377985} {:answer/id #uuid "1d88c06b-337e-5739-8d24-ac93a238915a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s+3)^{2}-16}{((s+3)^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377986} {:answer/id #uuid "c70c0c53-7d48-5b95-a0a5-62ae5b1b2703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377987} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb3f9f1-f1b5-5224-a8e2-206a5e5a673b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{(s-3)^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c211398-1c03-5d79-bc88-75ce8785ac37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377984} :answer-field/id #uuid "a52abd08-899a-5900-b030-9a02bc0ae94d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "601512ec-cb19-56e6-b7c8-01c935f6a50a" :question/math-academy-id "q-331869" :question/problem "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{4}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-{4}^{2}}{((s-3)^{2}+{4}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a921795-a527-5528-afcb-5881f188a665" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d7a3734-1dc5-5ee5-8061-9ff27cec1438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377991} {:answer/id #uuid "55142dcb-7399-596a-8681-57aad06734f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{(s-4)^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377992} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9eed3c-f51f-5899-87a7-2c0ba715c1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377993} {:answer/id #uuid "9964f37e-3584-50ea-82d1-f59d51d38a66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s+4)^{2}-9}{((s+4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377994} {:answer/id #uuid "a517c7ac-6318-57ec-93ba-07ebc1c85402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-4}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d7a3734-1dc5-5ee5-8061-9ff27cec1438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377991} :answer-field/id #uuid "088e17fb-065d-5075-be80-c3df5393a43c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9ec4f95-ebe0-557a-aaf9-41fafa0a7395" :question/math-academy-id "q-331872" :question/problem "Given that  $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377996 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe67aa0c-b2f6-50d8-baf6-d9d348d58f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $5$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377998} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4ebccc-3f40-5af4-a8bc-b755cbf85e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $5$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377999} {:answer/id #uuid "80901c91-7275-51e0-a013-ed8600020cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378000} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b5ddba-c0e3-5a85-a3ce-b8ff42c343c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378001} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb52edb-7547-59fb-81be-9c5810559fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1b5ddba-c0e3-5a85-a3ce-b8ff42c343c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378001} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f41402-758d-56a1-a894-e1df8c069c08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "810d3c65-0a30-5c17-ba06-443c4eded30d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332059" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{5t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{5t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bec80ab-d688-5ce0-af50-2663e85201d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378005} {:answer/id #uuid "10b12f48-b1b0-576a-9b7f-2e0276d21ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $8$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378006} {:answer/id #uuid "dccd8bc4-4632-5ec3-b8df-6745711fd0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378007} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f65e77-4dd8-5974-ae6f-5e3f3894787a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378008} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca9dc8b-3af6-5e3e-82e5-8600bc3fb012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $8$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dccd8bc4-4632-5ec3-b8df-6745711fd0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378007} :answer-field/id #uuid "80790c12-3444-5103-819f-37bdacc4be3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ca0b50b-5d4d-532a-8bbd-aad24e29bc44" :question/math-academy-id "q-332063" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{8t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{8t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9db4d32f-ad87-5954-890b-6574ab61e48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/3483a9ea106d12249b69b1214cd39770227db88fec4da8f430d2cbd2bed775c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378012} {:answer/id #uuid "15ee7be4-1eda-511f-8e82-c93aaa2dda08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/9ca5e0c34ea6514a73a1c16147b2c3e71bbf686fb874f92eb18817d3a8c6e0bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378013} {:answer/id #uuid "8889b759-f492-509d-9a87-7ac797ef8902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/6dcd65b95fb3bba3925833e4ceb4f2b7f1835312691083405a0958e3583fb128.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378014} {:answer/id #uuid "132af2f8-4eda-5f9f-b68c-ed57c4b69106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/966fab8b4260c34d381bed9b36a28e38711de8c77dc294c7a2c6f29d5cd76f36.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378015} {:answer/id #uuid "9f13336f-d5b1-55e2-8a72-5c665c4d8bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/3171a91443332410985bf7e42d54719185c884b9183e55e4ba7e574d6c0b2143.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f13336f-d5b1-55e2-8a72-5c665c4d8bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/3171a91443332410985bf7e42d54719185c884b9183e55e4ba7e574d6c0b2143.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378016} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8fb268d-4561-5478-b799-814aa1c91e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a148691-688d-5062-b592-e51f1bac7171" :question/math-academy-id "q-332116" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/9ca5e0c34ea6514a73a1c16147b2c3e71bbf686fb874f92eb18817d3a8c6e0bc.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L{{e}^{6t}f(t)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{6t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/3171a91443332410985bf7e42d54719185c884b9183e55e4ba7e574d6c0b2143.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfc05374-8f5e-526e-ac2e-dec8b6bac9d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378019} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cc487a-56e7-556f-b9e9-5cb13210c5a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378020} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6a1a56-37ef-548b-92db-53d6dffdef0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378021} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa2cebc-1ee2-5848-836e-c482688489a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{(s-3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378022} {:answer/id #uuid "476a0af2-cc7b-5b38-8b09-ccf3f76f2a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a6a1a56-37ef-548b-92db-53d6dffdef0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378021} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf03c69d-8514-5c63-85ee-d03362b60eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "349d4b15-ca25-58c6-9fd5-8cd3227b330d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332133" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=5{t}^{2}+9t{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{t}^{2}+9t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =L{5{t}^{2}}+L{9t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =5L{{t}^{2}}+9L{t{e}^{3t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL{t{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{(s-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{t}^{2}}+9L{t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01207b56-c7b7-5d71-b917-f9c259b96270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378026} {:answer/id #uuid "97f4ca32-717d-5b51-bf34-80479722f184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378027} {:answer/id #uuid "18167721-48db-5815-b5b3-d5c425ad198f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378028} {:answer/id #uuid "34eaced7-9b4f-5b9c-ada3-12ccb96a2376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378029} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9bd43f-3e89-5adc-833e-87c9cb9a2e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s-1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34eaced7-9b4f-5b9c-ada3-12ccb96a2376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378029} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bf1113-7bca-570e-9f5a-2b0d9d7450b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7eba2fe-279d-5114-9243-1203baad1a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-332183" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+L{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "728d1bd0-854f-5db8-972a-b2dcf7cb20fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s+2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378033} {:answer/id #uuid "22da784f-5fbf-5dfd-8501-d123f391ab58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{s-1}+\\frac{18}{(s+2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378034} {:answer/id #uuid "e50fba9a-42ec-5536-998b-8f7ba58a8073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{s-1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378035} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a2ead2-0349-5752-bc22-147593e0d8fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378036} {:answer/id #uuid "21adcb68-5bfc-5ff6-8479-1f92eebd73d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21adcb68-5bfc-5ff6-8479-1f92eebd73d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378037} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fc1a32e-0c5e-5f80-8d30-a7fe77f30ec0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea79698b-0d42-5638-aac5-5f80a3d13f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-332190" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{5{e}^{-t}}+L{3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1}+3\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315b5ab2-dc83-547c-bc5b-5ab2c8dc19d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378040} {:answer/id #uuid "65916f66-a1ca-5ca6-b9dc-2f67b42e2987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s+2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378041} {:answer/id #uuid "6a28d786-1b70-598e-9b8c-3d5af8c4a59f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s-4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378042} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecdffee-1d57-5815-ac2d-e5cc99d36fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378043} {:answer/id #uuid "749ab803-106b-5196-9236-17de17eb721b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "749ab803-106b-5196-9236-17de17eb721b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378044} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b1ef48-ad2d-5363-a807-789fa07277c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "025143ae-62a8-5506-b379-b3b2619c5b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-332199" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =L{6{e}^{2t}}+L{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ba61d-2e00-539a-84d0-84b63bf92be0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Graph Shifts From the First Shifting Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddd00a10-d84c-5e26-91e0-07cdcb69f2bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/afbe8291979d90666756181ca898851c5f0f30755ba985c32d56bd4b65e1fa5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378047} {:answer/id #uuid "28fd8864-3dac-5d8d-b61b-90d3064cb949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/15227beae514ccbfe85f3ba5d1ac4bd47fc130c5c15ff4ecd4de1a2813f8f2db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378048} {:answer/id #uuid "03783367-4a27-552c-9aff-ca63c29f73db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/6fd173c18910a9b2c428e7c4e5c52e13935a2439442610fea134ac9bb63fdf01.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378049} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb7d07b-195b-533a-bc16-44a8db22636a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378050} {:answer/id #uuid "50c81430-419d-543b-bda9-dbdbfd867a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50c81430-419d-543b-bda9-dbdbfd867a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378051} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e24b6f-17c2-5027-bd94-2dc045f1912e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e769abe-27f5-51cc-8d1a-a0d37ed70a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341512" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/ac72ad6572e3dd3b9b75c5d7a681ab14df055031cf3b1baa3c5b0df2de7ae5a4.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L{{e}^{-6t}f(t)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{-6t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the left.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072996/assets/fe5d17ffdb189defcd001b26eadf333df4d19157af44beeaa4c270083d1a625d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dd5c036-7a05-5ca4-aae9-1cf585839ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the First Shifting Theorem and Linearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "882e2401-4a01-5cf5-bb9c-e138532e0dd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/((s + 2)^{2}) + \\frac{2}{s - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220893} {:answer/id #uuid "8ec9e882-7755-5220-be1e-3c0532391c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/((s + 2)^{2}) + \\frac{1}{s - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220894} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0770f1-3bc7-5faa-8b6b-3608ed791e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s + 2} + (2)/((s - 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220895} {:answer/id #uuid "04c6f21a-d149-5481-af6e-b6e5e372daf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/((s + 2)(s - 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220896} {:answer/id #uuid "32921f3f-bac8-5fb3-8878-0d99ce52fc52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2)/((s + 2)^{2}(s - 2))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0770f1-3bc7-5faa-8b6b-3608ed791e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s + 2} + (2)/((s - 2)^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220895} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f65477f-53b2-50b5-be29-2c18caaf088a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94d7e94a-1d17-5470-a293-1353d69fb49f" :question/math-academy-id "q-89592" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+L{2t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-2t}}+2L{t{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$"}]}
